+	// tower: the `estimateContentLength` parameter is deliberately IGNORED, so a
+	// transcoded stream is sent chunked with no Content-Length.
+	//
+	// The estimate (duration * bitRate / 8 * 1024) never matches the real opus
+	// output byte for byte, and Go's HTTP server refuses a body that disagrees
+	// with a declared Content-Length: it aborts the response
+	// ("Error sending transcoded file: http: wrote more than the declared
+	// Content-Length"), so the client receives a truncated body and refuses to
+	// play (aonsoku: "unable to play the song, check the server"). Without the
+	// header the stream is chunked, which is what every client that does not ask
+	// for the estimate already gets; only the progress bar of the clients that
+	// asked loses its (wrong) accuracy.
+	//
+	// Upstream keeps the estimate and pads/cuts the body to match it instead
+	// (DevMatchEstimatedContentLength, master). Padding appends zero bytes after
+	// the last Ogg page and cutting drops it, both of which risk a decode error
+	// at the end of the track: this variant is the safer one.
 
 	if r.Method == http.MethodHead {
 		go func() { _, _ = io.Copy(io.Discard, s) }()
 		return 0, nil
